As I near the end of my seven-hour shift, a familiar soreness settles in the back of my heel. The checkout line is manageable, and I quickly spot Ms. “Kate Spade”. Her toothy grin is a warm welcome, reflecting the joy I feel in seeing her. She’s not the fashion mogul her nickname suggests, nor am I working in a high-end boutique. She’s simply a 71-year-old woman I met at the Giant grocery store where I work. I dubbed her Kate Spade after our first encounter when I noticed a beautifully designed floral glasses frame on her face. Complimenting it sparked a conversation about her love for the brand, and she even showed me her new purse. Since then, she’s become a regular at my line, and our conversations are always pleasant.

This summer job, though long and physically demanding, has been a source of joy for me. I cherish the brief interactions with customers and the surprises they bring each day. I’ve learned that not every discount arguer is simply obstinate; they might only have five dollars left on their EBT card. An elderly woman struggling with her Giant reward card might not be technologically challenged but simply hasn’t updated her account since her husband passed away last year. These encounters taught me to slow down and notice the people in front of me. 

Seeing a friendly regular invokes the same feeling of hearing a friend join a voice chat. ThroughaASDFADFASDF Community can also be something you create through small interactions with the people around you. I like learning people’s stories. I like finding ways to help.

That shift in mindset, from passively joining a community to actively building one, helped me see my passion for Computer Science in a new light. Early on, my focus was transactional: I entered competitions solely to collect accolades. But as I spent late nights in cramped backseats dissecting algorithms after travel meets, or in midnight Discord calls debugging code with teammates, I realized the true value of the field wasn't the scoreboard. It was the shared, messy process of problem-solving together.

I realized that the true goal of these competitions was never to find who was in first place. A contest is a place to learn and have fun. It’s a place to find like-minded people, develop skills, and work together to solve problems. I had spent years learning from people who shared algorithms, explained ideas, and stayed up late to help fix bugs. Now, I wanted to become one of those people.

When my Computer Security Club organized its own competition, I jumped at the chance to lead outreach. Pitching tech companies and asking them to sponsor us was intimidating, but their response surprised me.

Luna, the founder of Zellic, told me her company had started as a team of friends who met through cybersecurity contests. Now, she sponsors contests like ours to give back to beginners. Her story showed me the cycle I had begun to notice: people benefit from a community, and then turn around to build it for the next person.

At Giant, I learned that community can begin with simply paying attention to the person standing in front of you. In computer science, I saw how those small connections can scale into shared knowledge and opportunities that empower others. As I enter college, I intend to keep building that cycle and open doors for the next person in line.

